WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.923
RAII, Resource Acquisition Is Initialization, is the preferred C++ model for reliable cleanup.

2
00:00:04.923 --> 00:00:10.224
The resource is tied to an object's lifetime, and the destructor releases it.

3
00:00:10.224 --> 00:00:17.798
That means cleanup happens during normal control flow, early returns, exceptions, and partial failure paths without requiring every branch to

4
00:00:17.798 --> 00:00:19.692
remember a manual release step.

5
00:00:19.692 --> 00:00:23.478
Smart pointers are RAII tools when they express real ownership.

6
00:00:23.478 --> 00:00:28.401
Use std::unique_ptr when one owner controls a dynamically allocated object or resource.

7
00:00:28.401 --> 00:00:32.946
It makes ownership transfer explicit through moves and avoids raw owning pointers.

8
00:00:32.946 --> 00:00:36.354
Use std::shared_ptr only when shared lifetime is intentional.

9
00:00:36.354 --> 00:00:41.277
Shared ownership can be appropriate, but it also makes destruction timing less obvious.

10
00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:48.472
std::weak_ptr is useful when code needs to observe an object managed by shared ownership without keeping it alive.

11
00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:55.667
It helps avoid reference cycles and supports callback or cache designs where the observer should handle the target disappearing.

12
00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:01.726
It does not replace ownership design; it depends on a clear shared ownership model around it.

13
00:01:01.726 --> 00:01:03.619
RAII is broader than memory.

14
00:01:03.619 --> 00:01:11.571
File descriptors, sockets, locks, handles, mapped regions, and custom library resources can all be wrapped so cleanup happens in a destructor.

15
00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:15.737
That consistency matters because lifetime bugs often appear on error paths.

16
00:01:15.737 --> 00:01:24.068
When cleanup is automatic, reviewers can focus on ownership relationships instead of chasing every exit branch for a missing release or double

17
00:01:24.068 --> 00:01:24.447
release.

18
00:01:24.447 --> 00:01:28.612
Smart pointers help prevent use-after-free only when used honestly.

19
00:01:28.612 --> 00:01:36.186
Passing raw pointers from get() to long-lived storage, overusing shared ownership, hiding release operations, or creating cycles can reintroduce

20
00:01:36.186 --> 00:01:36.943
lifetime risk.

21
00:01:36.943 --> 00:01:44.138
The review habit is to ask whether each pointer type matches the real relationship: owns, shares, observes, or borrows.

22
00:01:44.138 --> 00:01:49.440
When the type tells the truth, stale access is easier to find and prevent.
